Synthesize the following 3 reviews into a meta-review:

- {"summary": "NAIT selects instruction-tuning data by aligning sample neuron activations with capability-specific activation directions extracted via PCA from small in-domain sets. It claims efficiency, external-model independence, interpretability, and targeted capability gains. Experiments on LLaMA-2-7B and other backbones show modest-to-strong gains over full-data IT and several selection baselines, plus cost savings and cross-task transferability analyses.", "significance_and_novelty": "Using neuron activation directions to guide IT data selection is a fresh, interpretable angle beyond surface features, uncertainty, or gradients. If sound and reproducible, it could influence targeted capability development and efficient IT at scale.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["\u00a73/Alg.1 presents a simple, external-model-free selection mechanism grounded in internal activations, which is conceptually elegant and potentially broadly useful.", "Table 2 and \u00a74.2 show consistent gains over full-data IT and competitive baselines using only 10% data; targeted GSM features yield the strongest average (37.70).", "\u00a75.1\u2013\u00a75.3 provide ablations on subset size, in-domain scale, model families (Table 4), different IT datasets, and interpretability/transferability analyses (Figure 5\u20137).", "\u00a75.2 reports large cost and time reductions versus LLM-as-scorer or uncertainty/gradient methods, supporting practical impact."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["\u00a73.2.1/Alg.1 lacks fully rendered equations and exact definitions (e.g., activation aggregation over tokens, normalization, layers used), hindering verification and reproducibility.", "Claims of \"significant\" superiority over strong baselines are weakly supported: in Table 2 the best NAIT setting exceeds SelectIT by only ~0.54 avg points; no multiple runs or statistical tests are reported (\u00a74.2).", "Cost/efficiency claims in Table 5 are hard to verify and may be unfair: Q2Q/SelectIT implementations and API assumptions are not specified, and 1.32h for 52k samples on A800 seems optimistic (\u00a75.2).", "Presentation issues reduce trust: method/equation rendering gaps (\u00a73.2.1), inconsistent naming/citations of LESS/SelectIT in Table 5, and typos (e.g., \"Oral-GPT4\" in Table 7) suggest quality lapses."], "suggestions": ["Report variance across seeds and significance tests for Table 2 and Table 4, and include per-task standard errors in the main paper.", "Clarify compute setup for Table 5 (sequence lengths, layers recorded, batching, hardware utilization) and align baselines\u2019 cost protocols.", "Add ablations on layer weighting/selection and on using more than the first principal component for V.", "Provide a small public demo showing how capability features transfer across new tasks to bolster \u00a75.3."], "rating": 5, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 2, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes NAIT, a neuron-activation\u2013based framework to select instruction-tuning data aligned with target domain capabilities. It extracts activation features from in-domain references via PCA and scores IT samples by alignment. Across MMLU, GSM, BBH, TyDiQA/XQuAD, and coding, NAIT reportedly outperforms AlpaGasus, Q2Q, and SelectIT using only 10% of data, with up to +4.65% on GSM and +3.24% average gains, strong cross-model robustness, and large cost/time reductions.", "significance_and_novelty": "Using neuron activation features to guide IT data selection is a simple, interpretable, and externally independent alternative to score- or gradient-based selectors, with broad empirical gains and cost benefits; novelty is plausible but incremental relative to representation/gradient-based selection", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Section 4.2 Main Results (Table 2/Systems 08 and 12) shows consistent gains up to +4.65% on GSM and +3.24% average using only 10% of IT data, outperforming AlpaGasus, Q2Q, and SelectIT.", "Section 5.1 Ablation Study (Table 4) demonstrates robustness across base models (LLaMA\u20112\u201113B, Mistral\u20117B, LLaMA\u20113\u20118B, Qwen\u20112.5\u20117B) with sizable average improvements.", "Section 5.2 Cost Efficiency (Table 5) reports 17.58\u00d7 speedup and 94.3% cost reduction vs SelectIT and up to 19\u00d7 vs AlpaGasus, supporting practicality.", "Section 5.3 Interpretability Analysis and Table 1 claim external-model independence, targeted ability control, and qualitative evidence that selected data emphasizes reasoning/programmatic features."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Appendix F Algorithmic Framework and Section 3.2 leave key implementation details under-specified (token pooling over sequences, layer selection/normalization, multi-capability combination), reducing reproducibility.", "Section 4.2 and 5.1 report single-point results without variance, confidence intervals, or repeated runs, so statistical significance of improvements is unclear.", "Section 5.2 Cost Efficiency lacks hardware/configuration parity details and may compare different selection fractions/pipelines, clouding fairness of cost/speed claims.", "Introduction/Table 1 claims first neuron-activation\u2013based IT selection, but positioning vs broader data selection literature (e.g., influence functions/other activation or representation selectors) is limited in the retrieved text."], "suggestions": ["Section 5.3 could deepen interpretability by attributing influential neurons/layers per capability with circuit-style analyses.", "Appendix I could add interactive visualizations of overlap and task-unique subsets to aid practitioners in mixing capabilities.", "Section 5.1 could include practical guidance on in-domain reference size/quality trade-offs based on Figure 2 trends. "], "rating": 6, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes NAIT, a neuron-activation\u2013aware framework to select instruction-tuning (IT) data for LLMs. It extracts per-layer activation features from small in-domain datasets (via PCA on activation differences) and selects IT samples with high alignment to target-capability activations. Across LLaMA/Mistral/Qwen backbones and multiple tasks (MMLU, BBH, GSM8K, TyDiQA, HumanEval), 10% NAIT-selected subsets outperform full-data or strong baselines and reduce selection cost substantially. Analysis suggests cross-task transferability.", "significance_and_novelty": "Introduces a neuron-centric criterion for IT data selection, targeting capability activation rather than surface features or external models. If robust, this is a meaningful step toward interpretable, efficient IT and capability targeting.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Section 4.2 Main Results: NAIT with 10% Alpaca-GPT4 surpasses AlpaGasus, Q2Q, and SelectIT and improves overall scores, including +4.65% using math features and +3.24% when combining capabilities.", "Table 4 in \u00a75.1: Consistent gains across LLaMA-2-13B, Mistral-7B, LLaMA-3-8B, and Qwen-2.5-7B, including large relative improvements (+21.92% and +18.65%).", "\u00a75.1 Ablation (Intensity of Neuron Activation): High alignment subsets outperform random (+3.35%) and low alignment severely hurts (\u221217.54%), supporting the core hypothesis.", "\u00a75.2 Cost Efficiency: Strong speed/cost reductions vs GPT-4o\u2013dependent and uncertainty baselines (up to 19\u00d7 cheaper and 17.58\u00d7 faster than SelectIT)."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["\u00a73.2/Appendix F (referenced): The exact activation alignment scoring function and token/layer aggregation details are not visible here, so key methodological specifics cannot be verified.", "Experiment Setting (LoRA warmup): A 3-epoch LoRA warmup to obtain feature checkpoints is introduced, but the data used and its influence on selection fairness vs baselines are unclear.", "OOD claims (Appendix C mentions MMLU-Pro, SVAMP, XQuAD, MBPP): Concrete OOD numbers are not shown in the retrieved text, so generalization beyond in-domain splits cannot be assessed.", "Statistical robustness: No evidence of multiple seeds/confidence intervals is provided in the retrieved results, leaving uncertainty about variance and stability."], "suggestions": ["\u00a73: Add visualizations of layer-wise contribution and activation-overlap across capabilities to strengthen interpretability claims without changing the method.", "\u00a75.1: Include qualitative case studies of NAIT-selected vs rejected samples to illustrate the hypothesized reasoning/programmatic features.", "\u00a73.2: Explore alternative dimensionality-reduction (e.g., CCA/PLS/whitening) to test whether PCA is essential or incidental to performance.", "\u00a75.2: Report normalized wall-clock and dollar costs per 1k samples and per model to ease replication across hardware."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}
